<header><p class="st-kicker">county · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Mac William Iochtar</h1><p class="st-meta">1333 CE – 1603 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Gaelicised Burke lordship of Mayo, ruled by the Lower Mac William, whose title survived until the late Tudor conquest of Connacht.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the murder of the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-donn-de-burgh-3rd-earl-of-ulster">Earl of Ulster</a> in 1333, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-albanach-de-burgh">Edmund Albanach de Burgh</a> took control of the de Burgh lands in north <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-connacht">Connacht</a> and adopted the Gaelic title Mac William Iochtar, the Lower Mac William. His descendants ruled much of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-mayo">Mayo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-mayo">Mayo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-burgh">Burkes</a> lived as Gaelic lords, electing their chief by tanistry, employing gallowglasses and poets, and allying with or fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odonnell-dynasty">O'Donnells</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omalley-clan">O'Malleys</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/clanricarde">Clanricarde</a> Burkes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-an-iarainn-bourke">Richard an Iarainn Burke</a>, 'Iron Dick', married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grace-omalley">Grace O'Malley</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composition-of-connacht">Composition of Connacht</a> in 1585 the title was abolished, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-mayo">Mayo</a> was shired. After Burke rebellions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-bourke-1st-viscount-mayo">Theobald Burke</a>, 'Tibbot na Long', son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grace-omalley">Grace O'Malley</a>, sided with the crown and was made Viscount Mayo in 1627.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mac-william-iochtar.jpg" alt="Mac William Iochtar" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Jacobfrid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MacWilliam_Iochtar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1333 CE – 1603 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edmund Albanach de Burgh, Richard an Iarainn Burke</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ireland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lower Burkes, Mayo Burkes</dd></dl></aside></div>
